      "summary": "The setting preserves the movement from distress and appeals for mercy, through the threat of death and exhausting tears, to confidence that God has heard and that enemies will retreat. Short lines and repeated petitions intensify the personal voice. It omits the superscription and the explicit healing petition, softens covenant loyalty into love, and adds images of fading, surrounding darkness, and attacking enemies. “Year after year” and “all along” extend the duration beyond what the Hebrew states. The ending retains fear and retreat but loses the repeated shame and sudden reversal. The Hebrew’s movement from the speaker’s disturbance to the enemies’ disturbance remains recognizable, though expressed with different English imagery.",
